+++
title: Palmyra
description: Ancient city in central Syria
hook: Palmyra reached the apex of its power in the 260s, when its king
  Odaenathus defeated the Sasanian emperor Shapur I. His successor, the queen
  regent Zenobia, rebelled against Rome and built an empire of her own — and
  in 273 the emperor Aurelian levelled the city.
standfirst: An ancient city in central Syria, in the eastern part of the
  Levant, where archaeological finds go back to the Neolithic and documents
  first name the place in the early second millennium BCE. It changed hands
  between empires before becoming a subject of Rome in the first century CE.
key-facts:
  - name: Where
    value: Tadmur, Homs Governorate, Syria, in the Syrian Desert
    from: |2-
        location: Tadmur, Homs Governorate, Syria
        region: Syrian Desert
  - name: Built
    value: In the 3rd millennium BCE
    from: |2-
        history:
          built: 3rd millennium BCE
          abandoned: "1932"
  - name: Abandoned
    value: "1932"
    from: |2-
        history:
          built: 3rd millennium BCE
          abandoned: "1932"
  - name: Periods
    value: Middle Bronze Age to Modern, with Aramaic, Arabic and Greco-Roman
      cultures
    from: |2-
        periods: Middle Bronze Age to Modern
        cultures: Aramaic, Arabic, Greco-Roman
  - name: The site today
    value: 80 ha (200 acres), ruined, publicly owned, managed by the Syrian
      Ministry of Culture, and open to the public
    from: |2-
        site-notes:
          area: 80 ha (200 acres)
          condition: Ruined
          owner: Public
          management: Syrian Ministry of Culture
          public-access: Yes
  - name: World Heritage
    value: Listed as the Site of Palmyra in 1980 on criteria i, ii and iv;
      endangered 2013–present
    from: |2-
        unesco-world-heritage-site:
          official-name: Site of Palmyra
          type: Cultural
          criteria: i, ii, iv
          designated: 1980 (4th Session)
          reference-no: "23"
          region: Arab states
          endangered: 2013–present
pull-quotes:
  - text: and the magnificent temple of the sun he (i.e. Aurelian) embellished
      with votive gifts from Palmyra, setting up statues of Helios and Bel
    attribution: Zosimus
    section: malakbel-and-the-roman-sol-invictus
  - text: a castle surrounded by a stone wall
    attribution: Yaqut al-Hamawi, Syrian geographer, describing Palmyra's
      residents around 1225
    section: decentralization
  - text: vast gardens, flourishing trades and bizarre monuments
    attribution: Ibn Fadlallah al-Omari, contemporary historian
    section: al-fadl-principality
  - text: Sparta among the cities of the Orient, Arab and other, and even its gods
      were represented dressed in military uniforms.
    attribution: Irfan Shahîd
    section: military
  - text: Palmyra was an Aramaean city and it is a mistake to consider it as an
      Arab town
    attribution: Javier Teixidor
    section: ethnic-identity-of-classical-palmyra
  - text: one of the most important single items of evidence for the economic life
      of any part of the Roman Empire
    attribution: John F. Matthews, on the Palmyra Tariff
    section: research-and-excavations
timeline:
  - when: around 2500 BC
    what: A mud-brick structure is built on the tell that would later carry the
      Temple of Bel.
    from: "[[ Archaeological sounding | stratigraphy ]] in the tell beneath the
      Temple of Bel uncovered a [[ Bronze Age ]] mud-brick structure built
      around 2500 BC, followed by different Bronze Age and Iron Age
      structures.[[ ^183 ]]"
  - when: around 2000 BC
    what: The city enters the historical record, when Puzur-Ishtar the Tadmorean
      agrees to a contract at an Assyrian trading colony in Kultepe.
    from: The city entered the historical record around 2000 BC, when Puzur-Ishtar
      the Tadmorean (Palmyrene) agreed to a contract at an [[ Assyrian |
      assyria#dynasty-of-puzur-ashur-i-20251809-bc-old-assyrian-empire ]]
      trading colony in [[ Kultepe ]].[[ ^182 ]]
  - when: 732 BC
    what: Aram-Damascus, whose eastern border the city had become, is conquered by
      the Neo-Assyrian Empire.
    from: With the rise of Aramean power in the Levant, the city became the
      eastern border of [[ Aram-Damascus ]] which was conquered by the [[
      Neo-Assyrian Empire ]] in 732 BC.[[ ^191 ]]
  - when: 217 BC
    what: A Palmyrene force led by Zabdibel joins Antiochus III's army at the
      Battle of Raphia.
    from: In 217 BC, a Palmyrene force led by Zabdibel joined the army of King [[
      Antiochus III ]] in the [[ Battle of Raphia ]] which ended in a Seleucid
      defeat by [[ Ptolemaic Egypt | ptolemaic-kingdom ]].[[ ^44 ]]
  - when: 64 BC
    what: Rome conquers the Seleucid kingdom and Pompey establishes the province
      of Syria; Palmyra is left independent.
    from: In 64 BC, the [[ Roman Republic ]] conquered the Seleucid kingdom, and
      the Roman general [[ Pompey ]] established the [[ province of Syria |
      syria-roman-province ]].[[ ^44 ]] Palmyra was left independent,[[ ^44 ]]
      trading with Rome and [[ Parthia | parthian-empire ]] but belonging to
      neither.[[ ^203 ]]
  - when: around 14 AD
    what: Palmyra becomes part of the Roman Empire, conquered and paying tribute
      early in the reign of Tiberius.
    from: Palmyra became part of the [[ Roman Empire ]] when it was conquered and
      paid tribute early in the reign of [[ Tiberius ]], around 14 AD.[[ ^17
      ]][[ ^44 ]][[ ^206 ]]
  - when: AD 32
    what: The Temple of Bel is dedicated.
    from: The !![[ Temple of Bel ]]!! was dedicated in AD 32;[[ ^137 ]]
  - when: "129"
    what: Hadrian visits, names the city "Hadriane Palmyra" and makes it a free
      city.
    from: In 129 Palmyra was visited by [[ Hadrian ]], who named it "Hadriane
      Palmyra" and made it a [[ free city | free-city-antiquity ]].[[ ^229
      ]][[ ^230 ]]
  - when: between 213 and 216
    what: Caracalla makes Palmyra a colonia, replacing many Greek institutions
      with Roman constitutional ones.
    from: "[[ Caracalla ]] made Palmyra a [[ colonia | colonia-roman ]] between
      213 and 216, replacing many Greek institutions with Roman constitutional
      ones.[[ ^237 ]]"
  - when: "260"
    what: Valerian is defeated and captured by Shapur at the Battle of Edessa;
      Odaenathus then wins a decisive victory near the Euphrates.
    from: In 260 the Emperor [[ Valerian | valerian-emperor ]] fought Shapur at
      the [[ Battle of Edessa ]], but was defeated and captured.[[ ^245 ]]
  - when: "267"
    what: Odaenathus and his son Hairan I are assassinated on their return from
      Asia Minor.
    from: The king and his son were assassinated during their return in 267;[[
      ^267 ]]
  - when: spring of 270
    what: Zenobia begins her military career, conquering Roman Arabia under the
      pretext of attacking the Tanukhids.
    from: Zenobia began her military career in the spring of 270, during the reign
      of [[ Claudius Gothicus ]].[[ ^276 ]] Under the pretext of attacking the
      Tanukhids, she conquered Roman Arabia.[[ ^276 ]]
  - when: late 271
    what: Vaballathus and Zenobia assume the titles of Augustus and Augusta.
    from: In late 271, Vaballathus and his mother assumed the titles of [[
      Augustus | augustus-honorific ]] (emperor) and [[ Augusta |
      list-of-augustae ]].[[ ^30 ]][[ ^281 ]]
  - when: "273"
    what: Palmyra rebels under Septimius Apsaios; Aurelian razes the city to the
      ground.
    from: In 273 Palmyra rebelled under the leadership of Septimius Apsaios,[[
      ^286 ]] declaring [[ Antiochus | septimius-antiochus ]] (a relative of
      Zenobia) as Augustus.[[ ^294 ]] Aurelian marched against Palmyra, razing
      it to the ground and seizing the most valuable monuments to decorate his
      [[ Temple of Sol | sol-invictus#aurelian ]].[[ ^291 ]][[ ^295 ]]
  - when: "634"
    what: Khalid ibn al-Walid takes the city for the Rashidun Caliphate on his way
      to Damascus.
    from: Palmyra was conquered by the [[ Rashidun Caliphate ]] after its 634
      capture by the [[ Muslim ]] general [[ Khalid ibn al-Walid ]], who took
      the city on his way to [[ Damascus ]]; an 18-day march by his [[ army |
      rashidun-army ]] through the [[ Syrian Desert ]] from [[ Mesopotamia
      ]].[[ ^299 ]]
  - when: "1400"
    what: Timur attacks Palmyra; the city declines after its destruction.
    from: In 1400 Palmyra was attacked by [[ Timur ]]; the Fadl prince Nu'air
      escaped the battle and later fought [[ Jakam |
      list-of-rulers-of-aleppo#mamluks ]], the sultan of Aleppo.[[ ^341 ]]
  - when: "1932"
    what: The villagers' relocation to the new French-built village of Tadmur is
      completed, and ancient Palmyra is ready for excavation.
    from: The relocation was completed in 1932;[[ ^358 ]] ancient Palmyra was
      ready for excavation as its villagers settled into the new village of [[
      Tadmur ]].[[ ^359 ]][[ ^357 ]]
  - when: "1980"
    what: The historic site, including the necropolis outside the walls, is
      declared a World Heritage Site by UNESCO.
    from: In 1980, the historic site including the necropolis outside the walls
      was declared a [[ World Heritage Site ]] by the UNESCO.[[ ^484 ]]
  - when: "2013"
    what: Palmyra is placed on the list of World Heritage in Danger.
    from: |2-
          significant-event:
            - value: list of World Heritage in Danger
              from: "2013"
  - when: 21 May 2015
    what: IS forces enter Palmyra in a sudden takeover; the Temple of Baalshamin
      and the cella of the Temple of Bel are destroyed that August.
    from: IS' forces entered Palmyra on 21 May in a sudden takeover,[[ ^365 ]]
      which abruptly ended the Directorate-General's efforts to evacuate the
      remaining artifacts consisting of large statues and pieces.[[ ^141 ]]
  - when: 2 March 2017
    what: The Syrian Army retakes the city.
    from: prompting an offensive by the Syrian Army which retook the city on 2
      March 2017.[[ ^373 ]]
  - when: 7 December 2024
    what: Palmyra is captured by the Syrian Free Army, a day before the fall of
      the Assad regime.
    from: On 7 December 2024, Palmyra was [[ captured | palmyra-offensive-2024 ]]
      by the [[ Syrian Free Army ]], a day before the [[ fall of the Assad
      regime ]].
