glossary:
  - term: Palmyrene Aramaic
    gloss: The language Palmyrenes spoke until the late third century, written in
      the Palmyrene alphabet.
    from: Until the late third century, Palmyrenes spoke [[ Palmyrene Aramaic ]]
      and used the [[ Palmyrene alphabet ]].[[ ^69 ]][[ ^70 ]]
  - term: colonia
    gloss: The Roman civic status Caracalla granted Palmyra between 213 and 216,
      which replaced many of the city's Greek institutions with Roman
      constitutional ones.
    from: "[[ Caracalla ]] made Palmyra a [[ colonia | colonia-roman ]] between
      213 and 216, replacing many Greek institutions with Roman constitutional
      ones.[[ ^237 ]]"
  - term: exarch
    gloss: "Lord: the title Odaenathus bears in an inscription dated to 252,
      unusual in the Roman empire and not part of the traditional Palmyrene
      governance institutions."
    from: In an inscription dated to 252 [[ Odaenathus ]] appears bearing the
      title of [[ exarchos | exarch ]] (lord) of Palmyra.[[ ^243 ]] ... which
      was an unusual title in the Roman empire and was not part of the
      traditional Palmyrene governance institutions.[[ ^243 ]][[ ^388 ]]
  - term: strategoi
    gloss: The generals who led Palmyra's military, appointed by the council with
      the approval of Rome.
    from: Palmyra's military was led by //[[ strategoi | strategos ]]// (generals)
      appointed by the council.[[ ^382 ]][[ ^383 ]] ... The //strategoi// were
      appointed by the council with the approval of Rome.[[ ^383 ]]
  - term: clibanarii
    gloss: Palmyra's heavily armored cavalry, its main attacking force; riders and
      horses were fully armored and carried heavy spears without shields.
    from: They used infantry while a heavily armored cavalry ([[ clibanarii ]])
      constituted the main attacking force.[[ ^37 ]][[ ^408 ]][[ ^409 ]]
      Palmyra's infantry was armed with swords, lances and small round
      shields;[[ ^223 ]] the clibanarii were fully armored (including their
      horses), and used heavy spears ([[ kontos | kontos-weapon ]]) 3.65
      metres (12.0 ft) long without shields.[[ ^409 ]][[ ^410 ]]
  - term: loculi
    gloss: The rows of burial chambers formed by the interior walls of Palmyrene
      family mausoleums, in which the dead were placed lying at full length.
    from: The Palmyrenes buried their dead in elaborate family mausoleums,[[ ^102
      ]] most with interior walls forming rows of burial chambers ([[ loculi |
      loculus-architecture ]]) in which the dead, lying at full length, were
      placed.[[ ^103 ]][[ ^104 ]]
  - term: ginnaye
    gloss: Lesser deities of the Palmyrene pantheon, popular in the countryside,
      who looked and behaved like humans and guarded people, caravans, cattle
      and villages — but, unlike the Arab jinn they resembled, could not
      possess or injure anyone.
    from: 'The Palmyrene pantheon included ginnaye (some were given the
      designation "[[ Gad | gad-deity ]]"),[[ ^427 ]] a group of lesser
      deities popular in the countryside,[[ ^428 ]] who were similar to the
      Arab [[ jinn ]] and the Roman [[ genius | genius-mythology ]].[[ ^429 ]]
      Ginnaye were believed to have the appearance and behavior of humans,
      similar to Arab jinn.[[ ^429 ]] Unlike jinn, however, the ginnaye could
      not [[ possess | demonic-possession ]] or injure humans.[[ ^429 ]] Their
      role was similar to the Roman genius: [[ tutelary deities |
      tutelary-deity ]] who guarded individuals and their caravans, cattle and
      villages.[[ ^420 ]][[ ^429 ]]'
  - term: Tariff Court
    gloss: The large rectangular enclosure south of Palmyra's agora where
      caravaneers paid their taxes, named for the 5-metre (16ft) stone slab it
      held with the Palmyrene tax law inscribed on it.
    from: The !!Tariff Court!! is a large rectangular enclosure south of the agora
      and sharing its northern wall with it.[[ ^131 ]] ... The court gained
      its name by containing a 5-metre (16ft) stone slab that had the
      Palmyrene tax law inscribed on it.[[ ^132 ]][[ ^133 ]]
further:
  - slug: dura-europos
    why: Palmyra kept merchants there from 33 BC, manned a garrison there in 117
      AD, and Rome's Cohors XX Palmyrenorum was stationed there.
  - slug: petra
    why: Rome's conquest of the Nabataean capital in 106 shifted control over the
      southern trade routes of the Arabian Peninsula from the Nabataeans to
      Palmyra.
  - slug: ctesiphon
    why: Odaenathus besieged the Persian capital in 262 and reached it again in
      266, without ever taking it.
anchors:
  hook: It reached the apex of its power in the 260s, when the Palmyrene King [[
    Odaenathus ]] defeated the [[ Sasanian | sasanian-empire ]] emperor [[
    Shapur I ]]. The king was succeeded by queen regent [[ Zenobia ]], who
    rebelled against Rome and established the [[ Palmyrene Empire ]]. In 273,
    Roman emperor [[ Aurelian ]] levelled the city, which was later restored
    by [[ Diocletian ]] at a reduced size.
  standfirst: "!!Palmyra!![[ ^1 ]] is an ancient city in central [[ Syria ]]. It
    is located in the eastern part of the [[ Levant ]], and archaeological
    finds date back to the [[ Neolithic ]] period, and documents first mention
    the city in the early second millennium BCE. Palmyra changed hands on a
    number of occasions between different empires before becoming a subject of
    the [[ Roman Empire ]] in the first century CE."
